SUMMARY:Eleni Sikelianos and Julie Carr: the William Corbett Poetry Series
LOCATION:MIT\, 182 MEMORIAL DR\, Cambridge\, MA 02139
DESCRIPTION:Born into a family of bohemians\, poets\, tree workers\, ne’e
 r-do-wells\, visionaries\, and smalltime sort-of hustlers\, Eleni Sikelian
 os is a poet\, writer\, collaborator\, and “master of mixing genres” (
 according to TimeOut). Kirkus has called her most recent book\, Memory Reh
 earsal (City Lights\, 2026\; the third book in her trilogy of ancestral en
 counters) “a moving family memoir and a triumph of cultural archaeology.
 ” Deeply engaged with ecopoetics\, her work takes up urgent concerns of 
 environmental precarity and ancestral lineages. Her tenth book of poetry\,
  Your Kingdom came out in 2023. Her work has been translated into a dozen 
 languages\, and she has in turn translated works by Jacques Roubaud\, Sabi
 ne Macher\, and\, most recently\, Mohamed Leftah. She grew up in earshot o
 f the ocean\, in small coastal towns near Goleta\, and has since lived in 
 San Francisco\, New York\, Paris\, Athens (Greece)\, Boulder (Colorado)\, 
 and Providence.\nhttps://calendar.mit.edu/event/eleni-sikelianos-and-julie
 -carr-the-william-corbett-poetry-series\nFrom Pushpin Boston: https://push
 pin.city/boston/
URL:https://calendar.mit.edu/event/eleni-sikelianos-and-julie-carr-the-will
 iam-corbett-poetry-series
END:VEVENT
